  "subtitle": "The pioneering non-linear editing system that introduced the timeline and thumbnail icons — built by Lucasfilm, bought by no one, and sold to Avid.",
  "why_scout_cares": "The EditDroid invented the visual grammar of all modern non-linear editing software: the horizontal timeline, picture icons (thumbnails) for clips, the bin metaphor, and the three-panel layout. It also features the most interesting custom physical controller in editing history — a KEM-style jog shuttle with trackball and context-sensitive LED-labeled soft keys that dynamically changed function based on the editing task. Only 24 units were made at $150,000 each. It represents a moment when a film studio's computer division — the same one that would become Pixar — poured a $40 million investment into rethinking a century-old craft through the lens of direct manipulation. The interface was designed by actual film editors, not computer engineers, resulting in a machine that 'felt like a Steenbeck' — physical muscle memory translated into digital workflow.",
